From streaming your favorite show to accessing cloud servers, your data travels through a web of protocols.

1. NAT (Network Address Translation)
•Purpose: Allows multiple devices on a local network to share a single public IP address for accessing the internet.
•Example: Your home router uses NAT to let your phone, laptop, and TV all use the internet via one ISP-assigned IP.

2. DNS (Domain Name System)
•Purpose: Translates human-readable domain names (like www.google.com) into IP addresses (like 142.250.72.196).
•Analogy: Like a phone book that matches names to phone numbers.

3. HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol)
•Purpose: Protocol used for transferring web pages on the internet.
•Port: Runs over TCP port 80.
•Limitation: Data is transmitted in plain text, making it insecure.

4. HTTPS (HTTP Secure)
•Purpose: Secure version of HTTP that uses encryption (TLS/SSL) to protect data.
•Port: Runs over TCP port 443.
•Security: Ensures confidentiality and integrity of data exchanged between browser and server.

5. TCP/IP (Transmission Control Protocol / Internet Protocol)
•Purpose: Fundamental communication protocol suite for the internet.
•TCP: Ensures reliable, ordered, and error-checked data delivery.
•IP: Handles addressing and routing of data packets.

6. UDP (User Datagram Protocol)
•Purpose: Lightweight protocol used for fast, connectionless communication.
•Difference from TCP: No guarantee of delivery, ordering, or error-checking.
•Use cases: Streaming, gaming, VoIP — where speed is prioritized over reliability.

7. SSH (Secure Shell)
•Purpose: Secure remote access to servers and systems.
•Use case: System admins use SSH to log into Linux servers from anywhere.
•Port: TCP 22
•Security: Encrypted, unlike Telnet which sends data in plain text.

8. TLS/SSL (Transport Layer Security / Secure Sockets Layer)
•Purpose: Encrypts data between clients and servers to prevent eavesdropping or tampering.
•Use case: Powers HTTPS (secure browsing), secure email, VPNs, etc.
•TLS vs SSL: TLS is the modern, more secure version; SSL is outdated and insecure.

9. FTP (File Transfer Protocol)
•Purpose: Transfers files between computers over a network.
•Use case: Uploading/downloading files to/from a web server.
•Port: TCP 21

10. SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol)
•Purpose: Sends emails from clients to servers or between servers.
•Use case: Used by your email provider to send your message.
•Port: TCP 25 (commonly), or 587 (for secure submission)
•Limitation: Only for sending, not receiving emails.

💭What is a Port ❓ 
A port is a virtual door on a computer or device that allows communication over the internet or a local network.
•It’s not a physical thing — it's part of the TCP/IP protocol.
•Ports help systems know which service or application incoming or outgoing data belongs to
